From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pf <xiaofei450@126.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 01:22:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Correct the in-flight comment: one thread per connection, not
 per request

NanoHTTPD's DefaultAsyncRunner spawns a thread per accepted socket
(ServerRunnable calls asyncRunner.exec() once per accept), and with keep-alive
that thread goes on to serve every request on that connection. The comment
claimed per request, which understates how long a thread lives and why the
in-flight cap is on requests rather than on connections.

diff --git a/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt b/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
index ac2d1b5..95679d9 100644
--- a/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
@@ -64,7 +64,12 @@ class HttpRemoteServer(
 
     private val json = Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }
 
-    /** NanoHTTPD's async runner spawns a thread per request with no cap of its own. */
+    /**
+     * NanoHTTPD's async runner caps nothing: it spawns one thread per accepted connection, not
+     * per request, and a keep-alive connection holds its thread until the client stops sending
+     * (an idle one is reaped by the 5 s read timeout). This counts requests in flight, which is
+     * what the endpoints actually contend for.
+     */
     private val inFlight = AtomicInteger(0)
 
     /**
